course: chinese-street
lesson: 24
title: Words that hurt — what they mean, never to be said
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: In every language there are words that are not teasing but punches; you learn what they mean so you know when someone is being cruel, and you never learn them to use — that is why this lesson gives the meanings, not the words.
explain: You can tell a real insult by ear even without knowing it: an angry face, a spat-out word, a "your mother" or "his mother" in a hard voice, or a crowd going quiet — that is the moment to walk away, not to answer back.
explain: Some Mandarin insults are pictures: a "turtle" said as a name is an old insult about shame on someone's family; calling someone "waste matter" means useless; a "nerve-disease case" mocks people with real illnesses.
explain: A few words are borderline: "méi jiàoyǎng" (badly brought up) blames a child's whole family, and "lǎowài" (foreigner) is casual and mostly harmless, but some people don't like being labelled.
explain: And in a British playground the worst teasing your Chinese friend hears may be in English: fake sing-song "Chinese" noises, pulling eyes, "go back to China" — that is racism, and a real friend stands with him and tells a teacher.
fact: One rude one-word "get lost!" is said like spitting in real fights; it is a real insult, shocking to an elder, and if someone says it to you, walk away and don't say it back.
fact: There is a rude "shut up!" built on a word for mouth; it is very rude to any grown-up, and the soft way in a friendly argument is "bié shuō le".
fact: Calling someone a "nerve-disease" case means nutcase or psycho; it mocks people with real illnesses and is not a funny word.
fact: Calling someone "waste matter" means useless, a waste of space; it hurts a lot, sometimes in online games, and some kids already hear it from strict adults.
fact: "méi jiàoyǎng" (MAY JYOW-yahng) means badly brought up, no manners; it blames the whole family, and if a granny says it about you, you probably forgot to greet her or interrupted — a "duìbuqǐ" fixes it.
fact: Asking "are you sick?" in a sneer means "are you sick in the head?"; among close friends it can be a light "you're crazy!", but it is easily heard as an insult.
fact: Telling someone to go and die is the harshest thing kids shout, in bad quarrels or online games; it is never a joke, and you should tell a trusted grown-up if someone says it to you.
fact: Calling someone a "turtle" is an old insult hinting at shame on the person's family; a real turtle at the zoo is fine.
fact: The most common Mandarin swear is built on insulting someone's mother and is used like "damn it"; any angry phrase about "your mother" or "his mother" is a swear, never to be repeated.
fact: "lǎowài" (LAOW-WIGH) is a casual word for foreigner, literally "old outsider"; people in China mostly use it without harm, but some people dislike the label.
fact: Fake sing-song "Chinese" noises, pulling your eyes and "go back to China" are racist teasing in British playgrounds, not jokes; stand with your friend, don't laugh along, and tell a teacher.
exercise: A teenager spits a one-word "get lost!" at another in a real fight. Is it a joke? | No, a real insult — walk away | Yes, a friendly goodbye | Yes, it means "see you later" | No, it's a polite request
exercise: Someone says that one-word "get lost!" to you. What should you do? | Walk away, don't say it back | Say it back louder, so he learns | Shout it at his granny | Laugh and repeat it to your mates
exercise: There's a rude "shut up!" built on the word for mouth. What is the soft way to say "stop talking about it"? | bié shuō le | zhāng zuǐ | xiào sǐ wǒ le | qǐng wèn
exercise: Someone calls a classmate a "nerve-disease" case. Why is it so nasty? | It mocks people with real illnesses | It's about his nerves before a test | It's a word for a good doctor | It means he's very brave
exercise: In an online game a player calls Lín "waste matter". What does it mean? | Useless, a waste of space | Very fast and clever at the game | A new player | A rubbish collector in the game
exercise: Lín's granny says "méi jiàoyǎng" about a boy who ignored her. What does she mean? | Badly brought up, no manners | Very clever, top of the class | Very hungry, not fed | Very shy, can't speak
exercise: Who does "méi jiàoyǎng" blame? | The child's whole family | Only the child's class teacher | Only the child's best friends | Nobody, it's just a silly joke
exercise: A granny says "méi jiàoyǎng" about you after you walked past without greeting her. What fixes it? | Say duìbuqǐ and greet her properly | Say the same words back to her, politely | Run away fast | Pretend you didn't hear and walk on
exercise: In a quarrel a kid sneers "are you sick?" at Lín. What does it really mean? | Are you sick in the head? | Do you need a doctor? | Have you got a cold? | Do you feel tired?
exercise: In a bad quarrel someone tells Lín to go and die. What should Lín do? | Tell a trusted grown-up | Say it back, just as loud | Laugh it off as just a joke | Keep it a secret from everyone
exercise: In a film an angry man calls another a "turtle". What is it? | An old insult about shame on his family | A pet name for a slow, gentle friend | A compliment wishing him a long life | A word for a swimmer
exercise: An angry driver shouts something about "your mother". What is it? | A swear — never repeat it | A polite question about your mum | A greeting to your whole family | A nice compliment to your mum
exercise: People in a Beijing market call Lín's English uncle "lǎowài". What does it mean? | Foreigner, casual | Old man, very rude | Tall man, friendly | Rich man, a bit jealous
exercise: Is lǎowài a slur? | No, it's casual, but some dislike the label | Yes, the very worst word in the whole language | Yes, it means "go home, foreigner" | No, it's a formal title
exercise: A boy in Lín's class pulls his eyes and makes fake sing-song "Chinese" noises at him. What is it? | Racism, not a joke | A funny joke everyone does | A friendly Chinese greeting | A harmless game of copycat
exercise: The same boy says "go back to China" to Lín, who was born in Manchester. What should you do? | Stand with Lín and tell a teacher | Laugh along so that it stops sooner | Tell Lín to ignore it forever | Say it back to the boy's family
test: my mate says if u learn the rude words u can shout them back, fair's fair. right? | No: know the meaning, walk away, never say them | Yes, fair's fair, they started it | Yes, but only at grown-ups | No, shout the English ones back instead, louder
test: Lín's best friend grins "are you sick?" when Lín puts ketchup on rice. Is it a fight? | Probably a light "you're crazy!" between friends, but risky | Yes, it's always the worst insult, whoever says it and however | No, he wants to take Lín to a doctor | Yes, he wants to go and die
test: Without knowing a word, how can you tell an insult is happening? | Angry face, spat-out word, crowd going quiet | Everyone is laughing and hugging each other tight | People bow and say xièxie | Grannies smile and offer you more food
test: A player in an online game types "go and die" to Lín. Lín says "it's just a game, it's a joke". Is it? | No, it's never a joke; tell a grown-up | Yes, gamers always say it, it's normal | Yes, it means "good game" | No, it means "you won", well done
test: Your English friend wants to learn "the Chinese swear about mothers" to sound cool at Lín's house. What happens if he says it there? | He shocks the family and offends everyone | Lín's granny laughs and claps for him | Lín's mum gives him a red envelope | Nobody understands it anyway, it's too fast
test: At the aquarium Lín points and says "kuài kàn!" and the Mandarin word for a turtle. Is that an insult? | No, a real turtle is fine; only calling a person it | Yes, never say turtle out loud | Yes, turtles are unlucky animals in every story | No, but only on Sundays
test: Lín's teacher hears a kid call someone "waste matter". The kid says "my dad says it to me". What's true? | It hurts; some kids hear it from adults, never add to it | It's fine because a dad said it, so it's allowed | It's a word for doing well | It's a joke in every family, nobody minds it
test: In a cartoon a kid pulls his eyes "to look like" the Chinese hero. The audience laughs. What's wrong? | It's racist mockery, not a joke | Nothing, it's only a cartoon | It's only wrong in China, not here | It's wrong because it's ugly
test: Lín says "lǎowài" about your English mum, smiling. Should you be furious? | No, it's casual "foreigner", usually no harm | Yes, it's the very worst slur there is | Yes, it means "ugly lady" | No, it means "kind lady from abroad"
test: Which phrase from this lesson can you safely use, and when? | bié shuō le, softly, to stop an argument | The one-word "get lost", to a granny | The mouth "shut up", to a strict teacher | The "turtle" name, to a friend, as a joke
test: What would happen if a child greeted nobody and talked over a granny at dinner? | She might say "méi jiàoyǎng", blaming his family | She'd give him a red envelope | She'd say "zhēn guāi!" | She'd say "xīnnián kuàilè"
exam: Identify the course of conduct appropriate upon being addressed with a vulgar Mandarin injunction to depart. | Withdrawal without retaliation | Repetition of the phrase at greater volume | Repetition of the phrase to an elder | Imitation of the phrase in jest
exam: It is given that the expression "méi jiàoyǎng" is applied to a child by an elder. Select the statement that accurately characterises its import. | It imputes a deficient upbringing, censuring the family | It commends the child's diligence and good manners | It enquires as to the child's appetite | It is a customary New Year blessing
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the term "lǎowài"? | A colloquial word for a foreigner, mostly without malice | It is a grave racial slur, invariably intended to wound the hearer | It is a formal diplomatic title | It denotes an elderly relative
exam: Ascertain the appropriate response of a bystander to the mimicry of purported "Chinese" speech or the pulling of one's eyes directed at a classmate. | To support the classmate and inform a teacher | To join in the merriment | To disregard the matter entirely | To retaliate with insults towards the perpetrator's family
